---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';

// The shape of a layout text, as the app writes it (docs/features-contract.md). Held here as a string
// because curly braces are markup in an .astro template.
const example = `{
  "krova_layout": 1,
  "layout": {
    "id": "mine",
    "name": "Mine",
    "zones": [
      { "id": 1, "rect": { "x": 0, "y": 0, "w": 0.3, "h": 1 } },
      { "id": 2, "rect": { "x": 0.3, "y": 0, "w": 0.7, "h": 1 } }
    ]
  }
}`;
---

<GuideLayout
  slug="layout-editor"
  description="How to use Krova's layout editor: monitor tabs, templates, splitting zones, dragging dividers, deleting zones, saving, setting the active layout, exporting and importing a layout as text, the space between zones, and the limits."
  lead="Draw, split and save the zones for each monitor, and copy a layout to another monitor or computer as text. You can try the editor on this page in the live editor on the home page."
>
  <p>
    <a class="btn btn-secondary btn-sm" href={url('/#try')}>Try the editor in your browser</a>
  </p>

  <h2 id="open">Open the editor</h2>
  <p>
    Choose <strong>Edit layouts&hellip;</strong> in Krova&rsquo;s tray menu, or start Krova a second time. The window is made
    when you ask for it. Closing it closes it for good, together with the embedded browser, and Krova keeps running in the
    tray. Unsaved edits are lost when you close it, so save first. Open it again from the tray menu any time.
  </p>

  <h2 id="screen">What you see</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Monitor tabs</strong> across the top: one per connected monitor, with its resolution. Switch with the mouse, or with Left, Right, Home and End.</li>
    <li><strong>The canvas</strong>: the selected layout, drawn at the shape of that monitor&rsquo;s work area (the screen without the taskbar or menu bar). A caption gives the size and the zone count.</li>
    <li><strong>The toolbar</strong>: Select, Split vertically, Split horizontally, Delete zone, Undo, and Revert and Save on the right.</li>
    <li><strong>The side panel</strong>: This layout (its name, Set as active, Delete layout), Layouts on this monitor (with New layout), Templates, and Export and import.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="build">Build a layout</h2>
  <h3>Start from a template</h3>
  <p>
    Templates are <strong>Halves</strong>, <strong>Thirds</strong>, <strong>Grid 2x2</strong> and <strong>Priority</strong>
    (a narrow zone, a wide one, a narrow one: 25%, 50%, 25%). Applying one replaces the zones of the layout you are
    editing. You can undo it, and nothing is kept until you save.
  </p>
  <h3>Or start from a new layout</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>New layout</strong> adds a layout named &ldquo;Layout 2&rdquo; (or the next free number) with one zone that
    covers the whole screen, and saves it straight away. Split it or apply a template.
  </p>
  <h3>Split a zone</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Choose <strong>Split vertically</strong> (<kbd>V</kbd>) for two zones side by side, or <strong>Split horizontally</strong> (<kbd>H</kbd>) for two stacked.</li>
    <li>Move over a zone and click where the divider should go. A preview line follows the pointer.</li>
    <li>With the keyboard: focus a zone, move the guide with the arrow keys (hold <kbd>Shift</kbd> for 5% steps) and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>.</li>
    <li>Press <kbd>Esc</kbd> to go back to the Select tool.</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Move a divider</h3>
  <p>
    With the Select tool, drag a divider. It moves every zone edge that lies on that line, so zones that touch stay
    touching. With a divider focused, the arrow keys move it by 1%, and <kbd>Shift</kbd> with an arrow by 5%. The outer
    edges of the screen are not dividers and cannot be moved.
  </p>
  <h3>Delete a zone</h3>
  <p>
    Select a zone and press <kbd>Delete</kbd> (or choose Delete zone). A neighbour that shares a <em>full</em> edge with
    it grows to take its space. If no neighbour shares a full edge, the zone cannot be deleted and the editor says so. A
    layout always keeps at least one zone.
  </p>
  <h3>Sizes</h3>
  <p>
    No zone can be made smaller than 5% of the screen in either direction. An edit that would do that is refused and the
    layout stays as it was.
  </p>

  <h2 id="save">Save and activate</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Save</strong> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd>) stores the layout. Until then it is a draft: the layout shows an <em>Unsaved</em> badge, and drafts survive switching monitors or layouts. <strong>Revert</strong> throws the draft away.</li>
    <li><strong>Undo</strong> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd>) steps back through your edits to the draft.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Set as active</strong> makes this the layout Krova uses when you drag on this monitor. It saves your unsaved
      edits to that layout first. Each monitor has one active layout; switch between layouts any time.
    </li>
    <li>Rename a layout in the Name box (up to 60 characters).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="delete-layout">Delete a layout</h2>
  <p>
    Choose <strong>Delete layout</strong>, then press it again within 5 seconds (the button reads <em>Confirm delete</em>).
    A monitor always keeps at least one layout, so the last one cannot be deleted. Deleting the active layout activates the
    first one that remains.
  </p>

  <h2 id="share">Share a layout: export and import</h2>
  <p>
    The side panel has an <strong>Export and import</strong> box. A layout travels as plain text, so you copy and paste it
    yourself. No file dialog opens and nothing leaves your computer.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Export layout</strong> shows the layout you are looking at as text, including edits you have not saved. Press
      <strong>Copy</strong>. If copying is refused, the text is selected: press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd> (<kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd> on a Mac).
    </li>
    <li>
      On any monitor, or on another computer, choose <strong>Import layout</strong>, paste the text and press
      <strong>Import</strong>. The layout is added to the monitor you have selected as a new layout, and the editor selects it.
    </li>
    <li>
      An imported layout is <em>not</em> made active. Choose <strong>Set as active</strong> when you want to snap into it. If the
      monitor already has a layout with the same id, the new one gets a number (<code>halves</code> becomes <code>halves-2</code>).
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>The text looks like this:</p>
  <pre tabindex="0"><code>{example}</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Zones are <strong>proportions</strong> of the screen&rsquo;s work area, from 0 to 1. That is why one text fits any monitor and
      any resolution, and why it should work on both Windows and macOS. Nobody has imported one on a Mac yet, because the macOS
      build has not been run.
    </li>
    <li>
      The format is strict. The text must have exactly the two keys <code>krova_layout</code> (the number 1) and
      <code>layout</code>, be at most <strong>64 KiB</strong>, have at most 64 zones, give ids and names of at most 64 characters
      with no control characters, and leave no zone side smaller than 1% of the screen. Anything else is refused, with the
      reason in the line under the canvas, and the text you pasted stays in the box.
    </li>
    <li>A monitor still holds at most 64 layouts, imported ones included.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="gap">Space between zones</h2>
  <p>
    By default snapped windows touch. To leave a gap, open <strong>Settings</strong>, then <strong>Windows</strong>, and move
    <strong>Space between zones (px)</strong>, from 0 to 32 pixels. The gap is left between neighbouring zones and between a
    zone and the edge of the work area. It applies to snaps by dragging, to the keyboard shortcuts, to windows restored after a
    reconnect and to the preview shown while you drag. It does not change your layouts, and
    <a href={url('/guides/edge-snapping/')}>edge snapping</a> ignores it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>At most <strong>64 zones</strong> in a layout.</li>
    <li>At most <strong>64 layouts</strong> per monitor.</li>
    <li>Krova remembers up to 64 monitors, including ones that are not plugged in.</li>
    <li>Layout text: at most <strong>64 KiB</strong>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Layouts live in <code>config.json</code>; see <a href={url('/guides/install/#uninstall')}>where it is</a> and
    <a href={url('/guides/multiple-displays/')}>how monitors are matched</a>. The editor&rsquo;s keyboard shortcuts, and
    the global ones that move windows between zones, are on <a href={url('/guides/keyboard-shortcuts/')}>their own page</a>.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
